# Level 1

Written October 21, 2026, after Bryan's second headset test. Level 1 is the first playable: one PC, one headset, one printed page, about three minutes a run. It is what we build and test next. The slice and the stages stay in the repo unchanged; what they add beyond Level 1 (the order lag, permits and codes, inspections, rain, field mistakes, money, the hub model) comes back later, one level at a time.

Decisions K29 to K36 in `Docs/DECISIONS.md`. Scenario `Data/Scenarios/level1_texas.json` (WO-L1). Rules page `Paper/level1_rules.md`. Headset WO-L2.

## The job

- 3,000 ft of water main in Texas. Stakes every 100 ft; 5+20 is 520 ft from the start.
- Three crews with one Standard machine each, in this order along the trench: Excavation (Ray) 220 ft a day, Pipe (Marcus) 180, Backfill (Dana) 250. A crew never passes the crew in front of it.
- The PM's plan: excavation starts Day 1, pipe Day 2, backfill Day 4. The PM promised Day 18.
- One thing goes wrong. When pipe reaches 5+20 (about Day 4) the trench wall there falls in, and pipe laying stops for 2 days while it is cleared. Then pipe goes on by itself.
- If a crew catches the crew in front, the two foremen argue and both crews stop until Plan holds the crew behind. This happens once per pair; after that a crew that catches up just waits, and the foremen grumble.
- A crew that has not started is not on site yet: holding it delays its start and costs nothing. Once a crew has started, every day it stands still is an idle crew-day.
- The clock runs 4 s per day, and 15 s per day while the wall is down or the foremen are arguing.

## The seats

| | Site (headset) | Plan (PC) | Rules (paper) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Sees | The site live: the crews, the trench, the fallen wall, the water. Hears the foremen | The chart: the PM's planned lines and what the crews reported up to yesterday | One page: what Site might describe, what it is, how long it lasts, the crew speeds |
| Does | Picks a crew and warps to it, looks, comes back, talks | Start, and Hold or Go for each crew | Says what it is and how long it lasts; works out when one crew will catch another |
| Cannot | Change anything | See the site, or where the crews are today | See the site or the chart |

Nothing tells the team whether a choice was right. The PM's voice pushes to keep every crew moving.

## Starting a game

1. On the PC, double-click `Play LOB Over.cmd` in the LOB-OVER folder. A window opens (leave it open) and the Plan page opens in the browser. The first time, Windows asks whether Node may use the network: allow it on private networks.
2. Put on the headset and open LOB, Over. It finds the PC by itself: over the USB cable if one is plugged in, otherwise over Wi-Fi with the PC and the headset on the same network. Campus Wi-Fi often stops devices from seeing each other; then use the cable or a phone hotspot. The Plan page says "Headset connected".
3. Give the Rules player the printed page (the Plan page links it).
4. Plan presses Start.

## In the headset

- You start in your room (passthrough) and can see and talk to the others. A panel within arm's reach shows the day and three buttons: Ray, digging; Marcus, pipe; Dana, backfill.
- Touch a button, or point at it and pull the trigger. Your room dissolves into the site and you are standing on the road beside that crew. You do not move; the world changes around you.
- In the site you look and listen. Foremen talk from where they stand; when you arrive, the foreman greets you or says what is going on. The only button is Back, on your left wrist. It dissolves the site back into your room.

## On the PC

One page: Start, the chart, and one button per crew that reads Hold or Go. Hold and Go act at once. At the end the page shows the finish day against the promise, the idle crew-days for each crew, and what the crews actually did drawn over the plan. Run again replays the same world, with the previous run drawn faintly underneath.

## How a run goes

Push, the PM's plan:

1. Plan presses Start. Site warps to Marcus and watches pipe go in; at the excavator, Ray says the ground is good and they will make their footage.
2. About Day 4 the wall at 5+20 falls in. Site sees it and hears Marcus get his crew out. Site describes it. Rules finds it on the page: a collapse, pipe stops for 2 days. Plan's chart will not show anything until tomorrow's report.
3. Dana's backfill started Day 4 right behind pipe. Within a day it reaches Marcus's crew and the two foremen argue: 250 ft a day against 180, and whoever wrote the start dates. Both crews stop. Site hears it and says so. Plan holds backfill, which ends the argument.
4. When should Plan press Go for backfill? Rules works it out from the page: pipe needs about 17 days for 3,000 ft plus the 2 days lost, backfill needs 12, so backfill can start well after pipe and still finish right behind it. Too early and Dana waits behind Marcus again; too late and the job finishes later.
5. The result: the finish day against Day 18, and the idle crew-days.

Pull, run again on the same world: the team holds backfill before Day 4 and presses Go when pipe is far enough ahead. The wall still falls, but Dana is far behind, so there is no argument. Starting backfill early barely changes the finish, because pipe sets the pace; mostly it buys idle crew-days and a fight. That is Level 1's lesson.

Measured runs (filled in by WO-L1 from `Monitor/test/scripts/level1_push.json` and `level1_pull.json`):

| Run | Finish (t, display day) | Idle crew-days (exc, pipe, back) | Argument |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Push: hold backfill at the argument, Go two days later | | | |
| Pull: hold backfill on Day 1, Go on Day 9 | | | |

## Voices

Foremen are heard only in the headset; the PM only on the Plan page. Each line plays once. An exchange plays turn by turn, each from its speaker's crew, 0.3 s apart, and exchanges never overlap. The texts follow `Docs/VOICE.md`: claims, not conclusions.

| Id | When | Who | Text |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `pm_open_level1` | Start | PM | Morning. Three thousand feet, three crews. Dig Day 1, pipe Day 2, backfill Day 4. We promised the city Day 18, so keep everybody moving. |
| `pm_hold_level1` | Plan holds a crew | PM | You're holding a crew? We promised Day 18. I hope you have a reason. |
| `pm_end_level1` | End | PM | That's the run. Look at where the lines ended up against what we promised. |
| `marcus_collapse` | The wall falls | Marcus | Three turns, as in `VOICE.md` |
| `arg_back_pipe_stopped` | Backfill catches a stopped pipe crew | Dana, Marcus | Four turns, as in `VOICE.md` |
| `arg_back_pipe_caught` | Backfill catches a working pipe crew | Dana, Marcus | Four turns, as in `VOICE.md` |
| `grumble_back_pipe` | Backfill catches pipe again, at most once a day | Dana, Marcus | Two turns, as in `VOICE.md` |
| `ray_working` | Site arrives at a working excavation crew | Ray | Morning, inspector. Good ground today. We'll make our footage if nobody bothers us. |
| `marcus_working` | Site arrives at a working pipe crew | Marcus | Pipe's going in. One-eighty a day if the walls behave. |
| `marcus_idle` | Site arrives at a stopped pipe crew | Marcus | Nothing to lay until that wall's cleaned up. My guys are standing around. |
| `dana_working` | Site arrives at a working backfill crew | Dana | Dirt's going in fine. Just keep me fed. |
| `dana_idle` | Site arrives at a backfill crew waiting behind pipe | Dana | We're right on Marcus's heels. Nothing to fill until he lays more pipe. |
| `foreman_held_01` to `03` | Each day backfill is held after it started | Dana | As in the slice |

## Later levels (proposed; decided after Level 1 plays well)

1. Level 2: orders reach a crew at its next shift unless Site is standing there, and Site gets a Stop button.
2. Level 3: permits and the open-trench limit, with Rules holding the cards.
3. Level 4: rain, the forecast and the pump.
4. Then inspections at zone ends, field mistakes (an order that never arrived), money, and the Stage 3 cities. The full design stays in `Docs/DESIGN_v0.md`.
# WO-L1 Level 1 on the PC

## Goal

Double-clicking `Play LOB Over.cmd` starts a Level 1 game: the Node host with `Data/Scenarios/level1_texas.json`, the Plan page with Start, the chart and Hold or Go, the printable Rules page, the foremen's and the PM's lines, and a host the headset can find over USB or Wi-Fi. The slice and the stages behave exactly as before.

## Read

- `AGENTS.md`, `Docs/LEVEL1.md` (all of it), `Paper/level1_rules.md`, `Docs/VOICE.md` ("Principles", "Triggers", "Slice line sheet"), `Docs/PROTOCOL.md`
- `Monitor/src/sim/*.js`, `Monitor/src/host/core.js`, `Monitor/src/host/server.js`, `Monitor/web/plan.html` (its chart), `Data/Scenarios/slice_texas.json`, `Tools/voice/`, `Paper/make_kit.py`

## You may change

`Monitor/src/**`, `Monitor/web/**`, `Monitor/test/**`, `Monitor/e2e/**`, `Data/Scenarios/level1_texas.json` (new), `Monitor/test/scripts/level1_*.json` (new), `Tools/voice/**`, `Voice/level1_texas/**` (generated), `Paper/make_kit.py`, `Paper/out/level1/**` (generated), `Play LOB Over.cmd` (new, repo root); additions only to `Docs/PROTOCOL.md` and `Docs/SCENARIO_SCHEMA.md`; and the "Measured runs" table of `Docs/LEVEL1.md`.

## Build

1. **Scenario** `level1_texas.json`, from the slice: the same job, crews, machines, start days, promise (Day 18) and clock (4 s a day, 15 s slow). One event, the P1 collapse at 5+20 with `durationDays: 2`, hitting pipe. No rain, forecast, deviations, inbox or protection. Permits issued and entered before Day 1, as Stage 1 does (`heldBy: "pm"`, no codes for anyone to read); inspections not required; money kept in the simulation but shown nowhere. The rule flags of step 2. The lines of `Docs/LEVEL1.md` "Voices".
2. **Simulation rules**, each behind a scenario flag that the slice and the stages do not set, so their transcripts stay byte-identical:
   1. A P1 with `durationDays` ends by itself after that many days.
   2. `rules.orderLag: "none"`: every order lands at once.
   3. `time.slowUntil: "eventEnd"`: slow while an event is active or an argument is unresolved, full speed otherwise.
   4. `rules.holdBeforeStartDelaysStart: true`: a hold on a crew that has not started moves its start to the moment it is resumed; until then it is not on site and accrues no idle time.
   5. `rules.planControlsClock: true`: the Plan seat may send `ctrl start` and `ctrl reset`.
3. **Voices**, only what Level 1 uses from `Docs/VOICE.md` "Triggers": a line may be an exchange (`turns`); triggers `event` with `eventType`; `arrive` with `crew` and `status` (Site dismounted within 30 m of that crew, at most once per crew per day); `conflict` with `pair` and `cause` (`leaderStopped` when the leader is not moving, otherwise `caughtUp`); `conflictRepeat` with `pair` (the trailing crew catches its leader again after the first argument; at most once a day per pair; nothing stops). `voice` messages carry the turns. Generate the new WAVs with `Tools/voice`, one file per turn (`<lineId>_<n>.wav`), cast as in `VOICE.md`, into `Voice/level1_texas/`.
4. **Plan page** `Monitor/web/play.html` (new; `plan.html` stays for the stages). Header: "LOB, Over · Level 1", the day, and the Site status: "Waiting for the headset", "Headset connected" (seat `site`, client `quest`) or "Site connected in a browser" (client `web`). Start, which becomes Run again after the result. The chart from `plan.html`: the PM's planned lines dashed, the reported lines solid up to yesterday, stations across and days down. One row per crew: name, foreman, speed, planned start, and one button that reads Hold, or Go while Plan holds that crew. Rejections in plain words; while the foremen are arguing, any command other than holding the crew behind is refused with "The field won't take that order right now", so Plan has to ask Site what is going on. PM lines play as audio, without captions. At the result: the finish against Day 18, idle crew-days per crew, the actual lines over the plan; Run again keeps the previous run faint underneath. A "Rules page" link to the printable page. Nothing else: no money, inbox, catalog, codes, radio log, JSON or debug tables.
5. **Rules page**: `Paper/make_kit.py --page Paper/level1_rules.md --out Paper/out/level1` writes `rules.pdf` (one letter page) and `rules.html`.
6. **Start script** `Play LOB Over.cmd` (repo root, Windows): checks for Node and says how to install it if missing; runs `npm ci` in `Monitor` when `node_modules` is missing; starts `node Monitor/src/host/server.js Data/Scenarios/level1_texas.json --discovery`; runs `adb reverse tcp:7331 tcp:7331` when adb (on the PATH or in Unity's Android SDK) lists a device, ignoring errors; opens the Plan page in the default browser; prints the PC's address and "Close this window to end the game". `--discovery` broadcasts `LOBOVER 1 <port> <hostname>` by UDP to port 7332 every 2 s on each IPv4 interface.
7. **Tests.** Unit tests for each rule of step 2 and each new trigger. Scripted runs `Monitor/test/scripts/level1_push.json` (no action until the argument, then hold backfill; Go two days later) and `level1_pull.json` (hold backfill on Day 1, Go on Day 9): record the finish, the idle crew-days per crew and whether an argument fired, and write them into the "Measured runs" table of `Docs/LEVEL1.md`. An e2e for `play.html`: Start, Hold, Go, the result, Run again, with `site.html` connected as `site`. A discovery test: a UDP listener receives the packet. `npm test` with the existing goldens unchanged.

## Acceptance checks

- `npm test` and the e2e pass; the slice and stage goldens are byte-identical.
- The orchestrator runs `Play LOB Over.cmd`: the Plan page opens; `site.html` connected as `site` turns the status to "Site connected in a browser"; Start runs a game to the result; Run again works.
- The measured table is filled in; the pull run has no argument and fewer idle crew-days than the push run.

## Out of scope

The headset (WO-L2), the GitHub Pages showcase, money on screen, and everything `Docs/LEVEL1.md` leaves for later levels.
# PROTOCOL.md — one host, one JSON-over-WebSocket protocol, three seats

Protocol version 1. The host owns the only simulation. Clients are thin: they render the view the host sends and send commands. The same protocol serves Phase 0 (Node host, browser pages) and Phase 1 (Unity host, Quest app, browser pages), so nothing a client does depends on who hosts.

## Transport

- WebSocket, text frames, one JSON object per frame, UTF-8. Path `/ws`. Default port `7331`. The host also serves static pages over HTTP on the same port: `/plan.html`, `/site.html`, `/rules.html`, `/facilitator.html`.
- Every message has `"t"` (type) and `"v": 1`. Unknown types are ignored and logged; unknown fields are ignored.
- The host sends `state` to every client at 4 Hz while the clock runs, and at once after any command that changed state. Clients never compute game state; they render.
- Times: `day` is a float in simulated days from the stage start (`0.0` = the morning of Day 1; the display day is `Math.floor(day) + 1`). `speed` is `"full"` (5 s per day), `"slow"` (20 s per day, during an unresolved event), or `"paused"`.
- Stations: feet from the start of the job as integers; the display form `7+20` means 720 ft. Zones are 1-based.

## Client -> host

```jsonc
{"t":"hello","v":1,"seat":"plan"|"site"|"rules"|"facilitator","client":"web"|"quest","name":"optional"}
// reply: {"t":"welcome","v":1,"seat":"...","stage":"stage1_texas","protocol":1,"state":{...}}
// A second client for a seat that is already taken is accepted as a mirror (read-only) and told so: "mirror": true.

{"t":"cmd","v":1,"id":"c-17","seat":"plan","action":"commit","ops":[ ...PlanOp ]}
{"t":"cmd","v":1,"id":"c-18","seat":"site","action":"moveTo","stationFt":720}
{"t":"cmd","v":1,"id":"c-19","seat":"rules","action":"ready"} // any player seat during a guide beat
{"t":"ctrl","v":1,"id":"c-20","action":"start"|"pause"|"resume"|"continue"|"reset"|"load","stage":"stage2_texas"}   // facilitator only
```

Every `cmd`/`ctrl` gets exactly one `ack` or `nack` with the same `id`.

### Plan ops (sent together in one `commit`; the host applies all or none)

| op | fields | effect in the simulation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `setStart` | `crew`, `day` | moves a crew's start day; applies at once; nack `"plan locked"` when the plan or the crew is locked, `"crew already started"` after its start |
| `hold` | `crew`, `fromDay` (optional, default the order's arrival), `days` (optional; absent = until `resume`) | flat segment: the crew stops at the order's arrival and bills while held |
| `resume` | `crew` | clears a hold or a stop-work on that crew (Site cannot lift a stop-work; any Plan commit after a stop-work lifts it for the zone's crews unless the commit holds them) |
| `lend` | `machine`, `toCrew`, `days` | the machine leaves its crew at the order's arrival, drives along the haul road (same day) and works for `toCrew`, returns after `days`; lending the excavation crew's machine to `pipe` during a collapse is the `clear_own` response |
| `order` | `machine` (catalog key), `rush`: false | buys a machine: price + $25,000 mobilization, arrives after lead days (+ city lead delay) |
| `protect` | `kind` (catalog protection key), `fromFt`, `toFt` | buys protection for a stretch; it is in place when the crew working that stretch starts there; wrong stretch = deviation F3 |
| `demob` | `crew` | the crew stops billing from the next day; `remob` brings it back after the city delay and the fee |
| `remob` | `crew` | see above |
| `pay` | `item` (an `options[].key` of an event that is active or whose forecast has landed, e.g. `"emergency_shoring"`, `"drainage_pump"`), `machine` (for `hire_excavator`: a catalog key) | pays for a response option at once; its effect is the event's authored one; nack `"no such response now"` otherwise |
| `enterPermit` | `zone`, `code` | opens a zone to excavation if the code matches the zone's card; a code not on file -> `nack reason "code not on file"` (a keypad rejection, not a judgement) |
| `enterInspection` | `zone`, `number` | clears the zone's hold point so backfill may close it |
| `radio` | `orderId` | re-sends an order Site reported as not carried out; it lands at once if Site is at that crew's station, else next shift; costs nothing but the clock |

An order (hold, resume, lend, order, protect, demob, remob) becomes an `Order` object with an `id`, a `crew`, `issuedDay`, `landsDay` (next shift, or `issuedDay` if the Site client is at that crew's current station at commit time), and `status`: `sent`, `landed`, `confirmed`, `notCarriedOut`.

### Site actions

| action | fields | effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `moveTo` | `stationFt` | the truck drives to the stake; arrival after `|Δft| / 1500` simulated hours (one zone per hour of game time; tune later) |
| `dismount` / `mount` | — | standing at the stake enables `inspect`, `flag`, `stopWork` |
| `inspect` | `target`: `"wall"|"water"|"pipe"|"machine"|"protection"|"board"|"sky"|"ticket"|"crew"` | the host returns `inspection` with the indicator text for that target at Site's station (from the scenario's ground data and current state); reveals hidden variants |
| `flag` | `stationFt`, `note` (optional, free text ≤ 40 chars) | places an unlabeled marker that appears on the Plan page's LOB at that station |
| `stopWork` | `zone` | crews in the zone halt within one simulated hour, keep billing, mood worsens each day held; stands until Plan commits (a commit lifts it for the zone's crews unless it holds them) |
| `confirm` | `orderId`, `outcome`: `"done"|"notDone"` | closes the order loop; `notDone` sets the order to `notCarriedOut` and appears on Plan's radio log |
| `listen` | — | at a conflict or an idle crew, returns the foremen's lines (`voice` with `lineIds`) |

## Host -> client

```jsonc
{"t":"state","v":1,"day":5.25,"displayDay":6,"speed":"slow","stage":"stage1_texas","view":{ ...per-seat view }}
{"t":"ack","v":1,"id":"c-17","appliedDay":5.25}
{"t":"nack","v":1,"id":"c-17","reason":"plan locked"}
{"t":"event","v":1,"eventId":"ev_p1","day":5.0,"zone":1,"stationFt":720,"card":{"title":"Trench wall collapse at 7+20","facts":["Pipe crew stopped.","Water in the trench bottom."]},"seats":["site"]}
// event cards go to the seats listed; the Plan seat learns of most events only from the daily report or from Site by voice
{"t":"report","v":1,"day":6,"feetByCrew":{"exc":1320,"pipe":720,"back":500},"spendUsd":31200,"notes":["Pipe crew: 0 ft today"]}
{"t":"inspection","v":1,"id":"c-18","target":"wall","text":"Wet clay. The toe of the wall has slumped into the trench; standing water 6 in deep."}
{"t":"voice","v":1,"lineIds":["pm_open_01"],"seats":["plan","site"]}   // clients play Voice/<stage>/<lineId>.wav if present, else show the text
{"t":"result","v":1,"stage":"stage1_texas","finishDay":37.2,"promisedDay":35,"budgetUsd":278400,"spentUsd":262900,"idleCrewDays":9.5,"peakOpenTrenchFt":1310,"finesUsd":0,"deviationsCaught":1,"deviationsMissed":0,"events":[...],"changeLog":[...],"lines":{"planned":[...],"actual":[...]},"previousRun":null|{...}}
```

## Per-seat views (what `state.view` contains)

**Plan** (`seat: "plan"`): `plannedLines` (per crew: start day, segments with slope from the machine rate, holds), `responses[]` (the `pay` options available right now: `key`, `label`, `cost`, `eventTitle`; empty when no event is active or forecast), `reportedLines` (actual feet per crew as of the last daily report; never today's), `budget` (`budgetUsd`, `spentUsd`, `committedUsd`, `deadlineDay`, `promisedDay`), `inbox[]` (dated items from the scenario plus daily reports), `radioLog[]` (orders with status), `catalog` (machines, protection, prices, lead days incl. the city delay), `permits` (per zone: `entered: true|false`, `number` if entered; never whether entering was wise), `inspections` (per zone: cleared or not), `flags[]` (station, day; no text unless Site typed a note), `draft` (ops not yet committed), `uncertainty` (ranges per crew from SR11-style bands; narrows when a report lands), `pmLines[]` (text of PM voice lines played so far).

**Site** (`seat: "site"`): `truck` (`stationFt`, `moving`, `dismounted`), `crews[]` (`id`, `stationFt` live, `working|idle|held|stopped|arguing|demobilized`, `machinePlate` ("Standard"), `moodLevel` 0-3), `trench[]` per 100 ft station (`state`: `undug|open|piped|backfilled`, `water: none|seep|standing`, `wall: sound|slumped`, `protection: none|box|shoring|slope`, `pipeDamage: none|cracked`), `boards[]` per zone (`permitNumber|null`, `inspectionNumber|null`), `sky` (`clear|towersSouth|rain`), `gate` (`trucksWaiting`), `activeEvent` (facts only, if Site is within the zone or has inspected it), `orders[]` landed at Site's current station awaiting confirmation. No plan, no money, no forecast percentages.

**Facilitator** (`seat: "facilitator"`): everything, including hidden variants, pending events and deviations, the two other views side by side. Never shown to players; used by Bryan during table tests and by Playwright during verification.

**Rules** (`seat: "rules"`): exactly `{stage, displayDay, speed}` in `state.view`. No positions, plan, money, daily reports, field events or result overlay. On welcome and reset/load it also receives `kit`: `{t:"kit", v:1, stage, title, job:{sign:{cityCode,pipe,contractDays},lengthFt,zones}, permits:{cards}, inspections:{cards,checklist}, manualPages}`. These are authored job sign, card and ledger facts, not live simulation state. The page loads the filled manual from `manual/<stage>.html`; signatures, checkboxes and ledger notes remain in that page and reset with the run. Rules can send `ready`; simulation commands are unavailable to it.

## Guided playthrough (WO-S7)

The Node host accepts `--guide Data/Guides/slice_guided.json`. The browser host offers Guided and a guide picker. Enabling Guided resets the run and turns Autoplan off; it can be enabled again explicitly. The selected guide must match the selected scenario. Without a guide, the existing clock and commands are unchanged.

On Start, and at each subsequent trigger, the host pauses and sends each player only their own prompt:

```json
{"t":"guide","v":1,"beat":{"id":"b0_brief","index":0,"count":9,"title":"Morning brief"},"prompt":"Find zone 1's permit card. Check the digits, then read the code to Plan.","rulesSection":"permits_holdpoints","ready":{"site":false,"plan":false,"rules":false}}
```

`index` is zero-based. Only the facilitator's message adds `narration`, `prompts:{site,plan,rules}`, and `beats:[{id,title,status}]`, where status is `upcoming`, `current`, `done` or `skipped`. No guide source or other seat's prompt is sent to a player. Between beats, `beat` and `rulesSection` are null and `prompt` is empty; player banners hide. The facilitator keeps the beat list. Joining a running guide receives its current message.

Beats trigger in order on start, authored event id, conflict pair (`back-pipe` corresponds to event id `conflict_back_pipe`), simulation time, backfill within 10 ft of a zone end (including idle at the hold point), or result. The host observes the completed simulation tick: conflict events carry the beginning-of-tick time and pause at the end of that 0.01-day tick. If a later trigger fires before an earlier trigger, the earlier beat is skipped. Triggers reached during a paused trip are retained until the current beat ends.

`cmd ready` marks the sending seat ready, broadcasts the updated ready map and acknowledges once. Once every connected primary player seat is ready, the host waits the guide's `resumeDelaySeconds` of real time, then resumes. Read-only mirrors and the facilitator do not count; no connected players means no automatic resume. A newly connected unready seat cancels a pending countdown. Readiness resets for every beat. `ctrl continue` ends the current beat immediately; any already-triggered next beat pauses immediately. Start/Resume cannot bypass a current guide beat. Reset/load cancel pending countdowns. With no active beat, Ready/Continue return `nack: "no guide beat"`.

Commands remain available during guide pauses. A valid Site `moveTo` while paused advances only the simulation travel duration (`|Δft| / 1500 / 24` days), including the fractional final tick, then pauses again. Other commands do not advance the clock. Guided Site receives only `{t:"result",v:1,stage}` at the result; Plan and the facilitator receive the full overlay. Rules receives the result beat, with its own prompt.

## Daily report timing

At each day boundary the host emits `report` for the day that just ended and advances `reportedLines` for Plan. Site's view is live at all times. This is the one authored asymmetry everything else depends on; do not "fix" it.

## Phase 1 note

The Unity host implements the same messages; the Quest client replaces `site.html`. The C# port is accepted only when the headless Stage 1 transcript (`state` snapshots at each day boundary plus every `event`, `report`, `result`) matches the Node host's transcript for the same scenario and seed within the golden tolerances.

